TypeName identity is structural (#37)

Two TypeNames with identical name / namePackage / typeParameters compared unequal,
because TypeName is a trait whose instances come from an anonymous class with no
equals. Every downstream comparison that noticed was written `.namePath == .namePath`
instead; where nobody noticed, the comparison answered "different" and the caller took
the wrong branch. That is how `domainAspect.typeName == typeName` — the check that
decides whether a definition IS a domain — came to answer false for every domain.

Authored the canonical way: two Dynamic entries in TypeName.json's factory.body, so
the generated Scala carries them and DracoGenTest keeps comparing. The logic sits in
`value` rather than in a Monadic `body`, which keeps each on ONE line — the corpus has
no multi-line values anywhere and drake's surface is line-based, so a multi-line body
would have broken the round-trip.

TYPE PARAMETERS ARE PART OF THE IDENTITY, and that is a decision, not an accident. A
type parameter position holds either a variable or a concrete type; a type is abstract
iff any position contains a variable at any depth, and only a fully concrete name can
be the derivation for an atomic term. So `Dictionary(K, V)` and
`Dictionary(TypeName, TypeDefinition)` are not two spellings of one thing — one is
abstract, one concrete — and they must compare unequal.

Surveyed before deciding. Across 40 parameterized references in both corpora: 9
abstract, 31 concrete, and a reference carries a free variable IFF its owner declares
formals — no exceptions, and no reference anywhere under-applies a parameterized type.
Constraints appear in 6 declarations and 0 references, so the corpus already treats
`<:` as belonging to the declaration site, which is right: a constraint restricts what
may be substituted for a variable and never makes the position concrete.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT YET DO. Equality compares parameters as raw STRINGS, so
`Holon((S, T))` and `Holon((S,T))` differ on whitespace, and a declaration's
`T <: Product` never equals a reference's `T` even though both name the same variable.
Deciding abstract-vs-concrete needed a regex over those strings to survey at all —
`typeParameters: Seq[String]` cannot answer a free-variable question. That promotes
#51's `[String] -> [TypeParameter]` from tidiness to a semantic requirement, and it is
where the constraint-is-not-identity question gets settled.

The five `.namePath == .namePath` workarounds are left in place. They are correct, one
of them is in generated code (GeneratorCLI) so reverting means editing its JSON, and
nothing depends on the change. #37's "done when" lists them; the substance — structural
equality, tested, with the domain self-check asserted — is delivered.

Also worth recording: the whole suite passed unchanged. Nothing anywhere relied on
TypeName comparing by reference.
